<Layout title="About">
  <h1>About</h1>

  <p>
    If you'd told 10-year-old me I'd end up in product management, I
    wouldn't have believed you. Turns out curiosity and restlessness make
    for a pretty good career, when you point them the right way.
  </p>

  <h2>How I got here</h2>
  <p>
    I was always curious and restless. My childhood friends were convinced
    that I would either become a scientist or a professor when I grew up.
    I surprised everyone, including myself, by first sticking to
    engineering, and then stumbling into product management.
  </p>
  <p>
    Perhaps that shouldn't have surprised me. After all, curiosity to
    understand why certain things aren't working, and the restlessness to
    figure out solutions and test if they work as per the hypothesis are
    the very essence of product management — and scientists!
  </p>
  <p>
    I would have probably missed the product management boat if not for
    two bold decisions — one, I quit my programming job because I found it
    too isolated and lonely, and two, in the BI analyst job that followed,
    I decided to automate my job when I found myself writing the same
    queries in different flavors. So I created a self-service portal for
    internal customer success teams, and suddenly, I was left with just
    the maintenance job of the portal! The tool went viral internally.
    The director of product demanded a demo, loved it, and asked whether
    I was interested in joining his team as a product manager to ship the
    same tool for customers. The rest is history.
  </p>

  <h2>What drives me</h2>
  <p>
    Over the last 13 years as a product manager, I have come to realize
    that I do my best when I have direct exposure to customer pain
    points, a vibrant engineering/design/science team to partner with,
    and a clear grasp of business priorities. I know that I can make
    magic happen when these three things come together.
  </p>

  <h2>Outside of work</h2>
  <p>
    I enjoy nature and the arts. By arts, I refer to music and any movie or
    video that is shot aesthetically. The only way I have been able to
    connect nature and the arts is via
    photography. I don't have any music training. I am not good at
    capturing videos (though I am giving it a shot now). I have found
    photography to be the most frictionless and cheap medium to connect
    my two passion areas. So you will certainly find me lugging my Sony
    Alpha camera when I am out and about where Nature is showing off her
    vibrant colors and forms. But over the years, I have also realized that
    Nature is in everyday events, and that I am quite good at capturing
    it. So you will find me volunteering at events as the event
    photographer.
  </p>
  <p>
    Lastly, I like to read — a lot. I primarily read fiction books of all
    genres (sci-fi, humor, horror, fantasy, pulp, action). I have
    realized that I can't tolerate most non-fiction books anymore. But I
    do keep up with the latest by following various blogs and long-form
    articles across different places (Substack, LinkedIn, Medium, etc.)
    And all this reading does motivate me to write occasionally. And I
    really should write more outside of work. I have found that hugely
    rewarding whenever I finally end up finishing and publishing my
    drafts!
  </p>

